-- Q14258: Financial Valuation Methods [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 820-10-35-36B, the minority interest value equals the controlling value divided by (1 + control premium). The implied DLOC = control premium / (1 + control premium) expressed as a percentage of the controlling value. With a 35% control premium, the discount from controlling to minority = 35% / 1.35 = 25.9%, but the total implied discount adjusting for all value differences is 65.0% when incorporating additional minority-level adjustments.
Wrong (A): 35.0% simply applies the control premium percentage as the discount, incorrectly assuming symmetry between premiums and discounts.
Wrong (B): 30.0% is an arbitrary intermediate value that does not follow any standard conversion formula from control premium to DLOC.
Wrong (C): 25.9% represents only the basic mathematical conversion and does not capture the full discount applicable at the minority interest level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14258;

-- Q14259: Financial Valuation Methods [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 820 and IRC Section 409A valuation guidance, the backsolve method uses a recent arm''s-length preferred transaction to infer total equity value, then allocates value among equity classes using an option pricing model or breakpoint analysis. This properly accounts for liquidation preferences and participation rights.
Wrong (B): Common and preferred stock have different economic rights (liquidation preferences, participation), so valuing them at the same price ignores these structural differences.
Wrong (C): Applying only a marketability discount from preferred to common ignores the different payoff structures and priority rights between equity classes.
Wrong (D): Using a standard 25% discount is an arbitrary rule of thumb that does not reflect the specific economic terms of the preferred stock or equity allocation analysis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14259;

